{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"exponent exponent","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md","fileName":"Exponent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Exponent","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Exponents and Roots]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Power","Zero Exponent","Negative Exponent","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"exponent exponent","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md","fileName":"Exponent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Exponent","heading":"Exponent","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An exponent is the raised value in a [[Power]] that specifies repeated multiplication or an extension of that pattern. In (a^n), (n) is the exponent and (a) is the base.\n\nA positive integer exponent counts copies of the base. A [[Zero Exponent]] makes a nonzero base equal one, and a [[Negative Exponent]] denotes the reciprocal of a positive power. Parentheses determine what the exponent acts on: ((-a)^2) squares the negative quantity, while (-a^2) takes the opposite after squaring (a).","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Power","Zero Exponent","Negative Exponent","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"exponent exponent","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Exponent.md","fileName":"Exponent.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Exponent","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","section